<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The one shared container the consented handoff moves through, and the only
	     entitlement this target has.

	     It must match the containing app's exactly. DEFAIKE_APP_GROUP_ID is
	     defined once per capability composition in ios/project.yml and applied to
	     both targets, so the two processes cannot drift onto different containers;
	     the two compositions get different values, so a pixel-only build and a
	     provenance build cannot exchange handoffs.

	     A build-configuration fact rather than a release approval: it grants access
	     to a container, and says nothing about whether this build may stage a
	     handoff into it. The container path is resolved through FileManager and
	     never constructed by hand, and an unresolvable container keeps the transfer
	     route unavailable rather than falling back to process-private storage
	     (AppGroupContainer, SessionStorageRoots). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(DEFAIKE_APP_GROUP_ID)</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Deliberately absent, each for a stated reason:

	       com.apple.developer.default-data-protection
	           Requirement 9.6 is satisfied by applying an explicit protection
	           level at creation, and the level is an approved artifact value
	           (ExtensionExecutionPolicy.stagedFileProtection) that the design
	           records against physical-device evidence. A build-level default
	           would be a second, unversioned source for the same decision: it
	           would either duplicate the policy value or silently contradict it,
	           and PlatformDataProtection verifies the attribute it requested by
	           reading it back either way. The startup gate additionally refuses to
	           accept work on a platform that does not enforce data protection at
	           all.

	       keychain-access-groups
	           Nothing is stored in the keychain. The handoff carries encoded bytes
	           and one bounded ticket through the App Group container, and both are
	           removed under the Data Lifecycle Policy's deadlines.

	       com.apple.developer.associated-domains
	           There is no universal-link route into the containing app. The share
	           extension point cannot open the containing app on iOS, and the
	           handoff deliberately ends with an instruction rather than a launch
	           (design, fixed decision 4).

	       com.apple.security.network.client, and any other network entitlement
	           The extension issues no network request. Requirement 9.2 forbids
	           off-device transmission of user images and image-derived data, and
	           Requirement 9.3 requires an Analysis Session to complete with
	           connectivity disabled.

	       com.apple.developer.healthkit, .homekit, .siri, and every other
	       capability
	           None is used. An entitlement this target does not need is attack
	           surface and an App Review question with no answer. -->
</dict>
</plist>
